us":null,"result":"I'm approving PKT-0116. The diff touches only three files, all under `.theseus/`: the new report `.theseus/oracle/rounds/docservice-r2-dbload.md`, the packet itself and one friction line. That is well inside the budget of 6 files and 800 lines. No code or tests changed, so the contract about existing tests holds.\n\n**Against the goal:**\n- **The run asked for was done.** The report shows `SYNC=1 e2e-convert.js 10 4` run against both the installed server (:80) and the worktree server (:8099), with 0 errors on either.\n- **Select counts and latency are recorded for each file-type pair.**\n- **The method is one the packet allowed.** The executor counted with `log_statement` and gives a sound reason for not using `pg_stat_statements` (it would have needed a PostgreSQL restart). Matching each query to its request by its `conv_k…` key removes background queries from the other server, which shares the database.\n\n**I checked the numbers against each other.** The average selects per request, multiplied by 11 conversions per pair (10 runs plus a warm-up), add up to about 161 on the original server and 304 on the new one. The report says 160 and 306, which fits. The largest increase is +5 selects on docx→pdf. That is within PKT-0068's expected \"up to ~6 extra\", and the report says so.\n\n**On the review rules:** the Executor log is filled in. It records the surprise that prepared statements appear as `execute <unnamed>:` rather than `statement:`, along with what was not measured (time per query, more than 4 parallel requests). No follow-up packets were asked for, so the empty Spawned packets section is correct. This is an investigate packet, not a characterize one, so the rule about keeping surprises in a test does not apply. The lesson is written into the packet's Atlas section.\n\n**Weak points that don't block approval:**\n- **The acceptance check is thin.** It only greps for \"taskResult\" in the report, so I checked the content by reading it.\n- **